course: dictionary
lesson: 3539
title: Federation: countries and groups that share
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A federation is a country made of smaller parts, such as states or provinces. Each part runs some of its own matters, and a central government runs the rest. Context gives the clue: «In a federation, states or regions run some things and the national government runs others.» The words "some" and "others" show that the power is shared.
explain: Federation is a noun, so you can have "a federation" or "federations". It also means a group of clubs, unions or organisations that join together, like a football federation. Both meanings come from the idea of joining together and sharing.
explain: Check a meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The US, Switzerland, Germany, India and Australia are federations.» If you put "countries that share power between a central government and smaller parts" in place of federations, the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A federation is a country made of states or provinces that share power with a central government.
fact: Federation can also mean a group of clubs, unions or organisations that join together.
fact: Australia became a federation when its separate colonies joined together.
fact: FIFA is the world football federation, and it makes the rules of football.
fact: «The Russian Federation» is the full name of Russia.
exercise: «In a federation, states or regions run some things and the national government runs others.» What does this tell you about power in a federation? | Power is held by the football clubs | Only one person rules the whole country | *Power is shared between national and state governments | Every state is a separate country with no link to the others
exercise: «FIFA is the world football federation.» What kind of group is FIFA? | A small team of players | A type of football shirt | A country with its own army | *A group of organisations that join together to make the rules
exercise: Which of these is named as a federation in the lesson? | *Germany | A town park | A football shirt | A rugby ball
test: In a federation, who runs some things? | *The states or regions and the national government | The children in each school | Only the football clubs | Nobody at all
test: Which phrase uses federation to mean a group of organisations? | a tall castle | *a football federation | a wet day | a sunny beach
exam: Why did Australia become a federation? | To split into many small countries | Because it had no states at all | *To join its separate colonies together | To win the football World Cup
